#{[17592186565314 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565314 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565314 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f7d207a9-c89e-548b-9f38-b94e8ddd223f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565333 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565333 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565333 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186565310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565333 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "127665a7-bfc2-524d-a3d5-c84c27346085" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565346 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565346 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565346 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565346 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565346 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565346 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565346 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565346 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186565102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565346 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "41f2e569-8706-5300-b37e-a72c9b91421e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565427 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186565333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565427 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565427 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565427 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+3y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+12y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565427 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4be05fa7-5d88-5239-abf6-50f8cd96f574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565427 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +7=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+3y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +3=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(3) & =24>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+12y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +12=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(12) & =-32<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565431 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186565346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565431 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565431 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565431 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=4\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c=2\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565431 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56b1314a-ceaf-5db3-a203-e533e79bae7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565431 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n4{y}^{″}=-2{y}^{′}-4y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}+y=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565491 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a0b64b5-8ad7-595a-ad6a-e5e032ce3417" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565491 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186357733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565498 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186565514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565498 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "32206203-4ed0-5734-9397-48990a5beafb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565498 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186348240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565514 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186565533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565514 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "772a7d55-73b7-505c-b338-1e17c49bf086" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565514 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186357720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565522 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186565498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565522 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99ee008c-724b-5795-b199-dd045f6be029" #edb/ref 1